d all that stuff that doesn't doesn't  mean I'm trying to diminish their  ability or what we need to use them for  is just that we don't train together  that's very important for people to  understand for us to understand how  they're gonna react in a volatile  situation how we're going to react what  is the standard of the law that  they have two parameters or if they  restrict it with so we try to utilize  them the best authority that is given to  them and I don't want to again  administer their help and their support  it's it's wanted and it has been  provided by the governor and we're  utilizing that resource I just want  people out there to know that it's not  the panacea in the solution to  everything that we deal with we have  seen a lot of peaceful protests here in  Las Vegas and as we've talked about some  in some cases they haven't been  unfortunately a lot of videos  circulating and people chiming in on  social media Sheriff as they do wanted  to ask you what the threshold is when  officers determine when the protest  needs to be ended by force or to  disperse the crowd well that's a good  question and it's a asked quite often  during these type of incidents and there  is no answer Alex it depends on the  actions and the counter actions of each  side of the fence so when I mean by that  we fully intend to allow people to  protest or demonstrate or express their  views and communicate and everything  they do in those type of situations and  I guess per se the threshold would be is  when they start to endanger the general  public the people that are outside of  the demonstrators or even the  demonstrators themselves and or law  enforcement with that being said there's  a dispersal order when say the the  commander of that particular incident  makes a decision a this is starting to  get dangerous or impeding the general  public and their ability to go about  their lives there they're causing harm  by throwing rocks bottles missiles  anything you want to describe in that  effect and it's time for us to provide a  dispersal order and time for them to  move on and cease the event and what I  mean by the dispersal order is it's a  verbal personal orders given by a  commander out on the scene usually be a  bullhorn and it has to be three times  with ten minutes in between each other  so about thirty minutes  they give the people the opportunity to  lead they've been warmed sufficiently  it's time for you to leave and go about  your business and what has happened in  that anatomy of these protests a day  over day is eventually make the decision  it's time to disperse them we give the  dispersal orders and that's when the  rocks and everything else start to fly  with our zest I would to say so at that  point we identify the key agitators and  attempt to get them into custody and  hope to quell the violence that's what  happened with officer Michael anus you  know at that point we gave the dispersal  order we wanted people to put into  custody and then the tragic events  associated with that  well sheriff you mentioned when those  arrests take place that the officers try  and look for the the agitators we've  we've seen a lot of arrests that have  taken place we've seen the use of tear  gas a pepper spray  not only at in some cases peaceful  members of the protest but also members  of the media are are you at all somewhat  concerned critical or are you pleased  with the actions it's some of the more  highly charged moments of these protests  well that's a good good point but here's  here's a deal a lot of times when you've  seen that there's a perception maybe  they're peaceful or they're docile you  usually don't see the back end of what's  taking place prior to the effect of the  rest but including the net even though  it may have perception that they're  docile and they're not the ones actually  throwing the rocks per se they might be  encompassed in the dispersal order so by  the fact that they're remaining in place  refusing to move they may be part of  that group and and once the disperse  order is given and it's and you have not  let left you are violating the law and  that's basically failure to disperse so  those journalists that's you are or  pining on or discussing or describing  they were caught up in that type of fray  and so the officers didn't have the  ability to determine whether they were  journalists at the time this is what I  my understanding you know the answer  obviously a playout record I'm sure but  that was part of that type of incident  or they're caught in the frame they  refused  those first orders sheriff in the last  segment you talked about some of the  black guys from Metro Police in the past  there have been efforts to try and  address that through federal court  procedures the ACLU etc in the community  if one of the the crux of some of these  protests is is social justice equality  and and police accountability some would  suggest our still issues with Metro when  it comes to public records when it comes  to dealing with some members of the  community use of certain kinds of choke  holds and other efforts within the  department don't those also have to be  addressed in order to sort of try and  appease all parts of the community yeah  you're absolutely right and you haven't  described or said one particular type of  issue that we haven't addressed or we  haven't freedom compromised or got in  discussion with or disgusting the court  system on each one of those so I don't  think as an organization we've missed  any particular incident or type of the  policy or procedure that we need to  address and when I say black I yeah  you're exactly right we failed to act  previously but in today's age and in  current time I think we've been pretty  successful and a lot of that is  cooperation with the public and dealing  with outside organizations to develop  that compromise and I'm very proud of  our robust relationship with the  community in particular communities of  different races and cultures the metric  metro multicultural Advisory Committee  has been in place and it has very robust  power within my organization what they  quite often bring those particular type  issues forward and we're happy to  discuss with them compromise and change  how we do business  sheriff we only have a few seconds left  but want to ask you really quickly a lot  of resorts on the strip are reopening  tomorrow  how will Metro be involved with this  especially as these protests continue  well first and foremost  I have to applaud that they're opening I  think we need it I think people need a  released economy needs a release and you  know one of them unfortunate things of  us there's their civil and I would say  sober arrest is this this protests and  demonstrations is the code of nineteen  is fall on the backside and I think  people have lost sight of that it's  still out there and we have to address  it continuing to move in forward but  with that said I'm glad the resorts are  opening we've been in constant  conversation with their security  apparatus as a police department should  be in these type of situations we're  given advice both ways on how we should  do this going forward in conjunction  with the Gaming Control Board and all  the preventive measures put on on the  floor space of these casinos so I guess  to answer your question Alex it's been a  robust communication back and forth and  hopefully we're gonna be back with  success here tomorrow upon the grand  openings and then that our economy has a  release for our local residents inspires  unemployment and anxiety and everything  else that we deal with on a daily basis  as a community which may be part and  parcel to some of the frustration of  people participating in these  demonstrations and we're not you're all  about mr. Floyd on its own and social  injustice on its own it may be about  just what the hell is going on in our  lives here recently all right sheriff we  are all out of time unfortunately thanks  for taking a time we appreciate it and  we send our very best to officer Michael  onus thank you very much

